pub(super) const NO_OVEN_BREAD: &str = "Bread with no oven is flatbread: flour, water, salt and a little oil, rested 20 minutes, rolled thin and cooked in a dry hot pan for two minutes a side. The same dough pushed into a hot oiled frying pan, lid on, turned once, makes a thick loaf that will do for toast.";
pub(super) const NO_BAKING: &str = "You cannot bake without an oven, but a fridge cake needs none: melt 100g butter, 200g chocolate and 2 tablespoons of golden syrup in a pan, stir in 200g crushed biscuits and a handful of raisins, press it into a tin and chill it for 2 hours. A cheesecake that sets in the fridge, tiramisu and lemon posset are the same trick: they set cold, not in the oven.";
pub(super) const NO_OVEN_ONLY: &str = "With an oven and no hob, cook in one tray: chicken thighs or sausages with chopped potato, onion and pepper, oil, salt and herbs, 40 minutes at 200C (180C fan), turned once. Jacket potatoes take an hour at the same heat, baked pasta or a tin of tomatoes with butter and breadcrumbs bakes in 25 minutes, and roasted veg with a drained tin of chickpeas makes a meal on its own.";
pub(super) const NO_HOB: &str = "With only a microwave you can still eat properly: jacket potatoes (10 minutes, turned once), rice or pasta cooked in a big bowl of water, porridge, scrambled eggs, steamed frozen veg, tinned soup or beans, and a poached egg in a mug of water. Give a jacket potato a rub of oil and salt first and it comes out closer to oven-baked.";
pub(super) const NO_KETTLE: &str = "A kettle still makes a real meal out of couscous: put it in a bowl, pour boiling water over to just cover it, cover the bowl for five minutes, then fork it through with tinned chickpeas or tuna, chopped tomato, olives and lemon. Overnight oats (soak them in the fridge), instant porridge, pot noodles and cup soup need nothing more. If you can stretch to a cheap single-ring hob, it opens up pasta, rice, eggs and tinned soup.";
pub(super) const NO_OVEN: &str = "With a hob you can still eat well: pasta with a sauce made in the pan, rice with stir-fried veg and an egg on top, a one-pan curry of chickpeas or lentils with tomatoes, quesadillas or toasted sandwiches pressed in a dry pan, or potatoes boiled and then crushed into a pan with butter and greens. Eggs, tinned fish and beans all cook in a pan in minutes.";
pub(super) const NO_MICROWAVE: &str = "Without a microwave, a pan does the same jobs: reheat rice or leftovers with a splash of water over a low heat, defrost in the fridge overnight, and cook porridge or scrambled eggs in a saucepan in about five minutes.";
pub(super) const BATTER_REPLY: &str = "A {name} is a pan job as it is: the batter goes into a hot non-stick pan with a little butter, about a minute a side until bubbles pop and the edges set. Crumpets want rings in a dry pan on a low heat until the top sets and the holes stay open. What will not work on a hob is anything that has to rise and brown in a tin - a sponge, a loaf, pastry - and those are the no-bake answers instead.";
pub(super) const DOUGH_REPLY: &str = "A {name} cooks in a heavy dry pan, not a tin: the same dough made a little thicker and patted out, about 5 minutes a side on a low heat with a lid on, until the middle is set and the edges have caught. Scones, pikelets and roti are the same trick. What will not work on a hob is anything that has to rise and brown in a tin - a sponge, a loaf, pastry - and those are the no-bake answers instead.";
pub(super) const SCONE_REPLY: &str = "A scone cooks in a heavy pan: the same dough made a little thicker and patted out, in a dry pan on a low heat with a lid on for about 5 minutes a side, until the middle is set and the edges have caught. Drop scones, pikelets, chapatis and roti are the same trick on a hot dry pan. What will not work on a hob is anything that has to rise and brown in a tin - a sponge, a loaf, pastry - and those are the no-bake answers instead.";
pub(super) const WRAP_REPLY: &str = "A {name} is warmed rather than baked: 30 seconds a side in a dry hot pan and it is soft enough to fold, and a pitta puffs the same way. What will not work on a hob is anything that has to rise and brown in a tin - a sponge, a loaf, pastry - and those are the no-bake answers instead.";
pub(super) const PITTA_REPLY: &str = "A {name} is warmed rather than baked: 30 seconds a side in a dry hot pan and it is soft enough to fold, and a wrap warms the same way. What will not work on a hob is anything that has to rise and brown in a tin - a sponge, a loaf, pastry - and those are the no-bake answers instead.";
